<!-- Website teaching layer; original time-and-clocks.md remains unchanged. Primary-source review: batch-3.json. -->
<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">An upload starts at 10:00:00. The device corrects its clock backward while the request is running. The duration metric becomes negative, although the network request still took time. The timestamp answered “what time is it?” when the code needed “how long have I waited?”</p>

Choose the question before the clock: calendar instant, elapsed interval or causal precedence. These are different contracts. Greater numeric precision does not turn one into another.

<!-- @before Internal Mechanics :: guide -->
### Keep the origin with the value

| Requirement | Appropriate starting point | Boundary to keep explicit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Display when a receipt was issued | An authoritative calendar instant | Timezone changes its presentation; clock adjustments can change later readings |
| Measure one JVM operation | Differences from `nanoTime`, or `kotlin.time.measureTime` | Both readings belong to the same time source/JVM lifetime |
| Android timeout that includes deep sleep | `elapsedRealtime` differences | Values restart at boot; this does not make wakeups exact |
| Explain which message caused an event | Message-aware logical timestamps | Lamport timestamp order alone does not prove causality in reverse |

[Sources: Java elapsed-time contract](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/System.html#nanoTime()), [Android clock and sleep behavior](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/os/SystemClock).

<!-- @after Architecture :: animation -->
### Only messages carry logical time between processes

<section class="pb-figure" data-pb-flow="scene" aria-label="Local ticks stay local; messages carry timestamps">
<h3>Local ticks stay local; messages carry timestamps</h3><p class="pb-sub">Illustrative values and order, not measured timings.</p>

