<script type="application/json">{"illustrated": true, "nodes": [{"id": "a", "label": "Process A", "art": {"kind": "server", "role": "client"}}, {"id": "b", "label": "Process B", "art": {"kind": "server", "role": "runtime"}}], "layouts": {"wide": {"size": [760, 330], "nodes": {"a": {"x": 24, "y": 80, "w": 270, "h": 166}, "b": {"x": 450, "y": 80, "w": 270, "h": 166}}, "edges": {"ab": "M296 130 C340 130 404 130 448 130", "ba": "M448 205 C404 205 340 205 296 205"}, "labels": [{"x": 372, "y": 57, "text": "A sends a message to B"}, {"x": 372, "y": 285, "text": "B replies to A"}], "pk": {"dx": 0, "dy": -17, "anchor": "middle"}}, "narrow": {"size": [760, 330], "nodes": {"a": {"x": 24, "y": 80, "w": 270, "h": 166}, "b": {"x": 450, "y": 80, "w": 270, "h": 166}}, "edges": {"ab": "M296 130 C340 130 404 130 448 130", "ba": "M448 205 C404 205 340 205 296 205"}, "labels": [{"x": 372, "y": 57, "text": "A sends a message to B"}, {"x": 372, "y": 285, "text": "B replies to A"}], "pk": {"dx": 0, "dy": -17, "anchor": "middle"}}}, "modes": {"run": {"initial": {"nodes": {"a": ["", "LC = 0"], "b": ["", "LC = 0"]}}, "steps": [{"caption": "Both counters start at zero. These are logical counters, not seconds.", "set": {"nodes": {"a": ["", "LC = 0"], "b": ["", "LC = 0"]}}}, {"caption": "A performs a local event and increments to 1. Nothing is transmitted to B.", "set": {"nodes": {"a": ["is-active", "LC = 1"]}}}, {"caption": "A’s send event increments to 2. Its message carries 2; B receives and sets max(0, 2) + 1 = 3.", "set": {"nodes": {"a": ["is-done", "LC = 2"], "b": ["is-active", "LC = 3"]}}, "packets": [{"edge": "ab", "label": "message: LC 2", "tone": "fwd", "kind": "page"}]}, {"caption": "B performs a local event and increments from 3 to 4. A remains at 2.", "set": {"nodes": {"b": ["is-active", "LC = 4"]}}}, {"caption": "B’s reply increments to 5. A receives that timestamp and sets max(2, 5) + 1 = 6.", "set": {"nodes": {"b": ["is-done", "LC = 5"], "a": ["is-active", "LC = 6"]}}, "packets": [{"edge": "ba", "label": "reply: LC 5", "tone": "back", "kind": "page"}]}]}}}</script>
</section>

A local tick stays local. A send increments the sender; a receive merges the transmitted timestamp and increments the receiver. The result preserves causal precedence even when physical clocks disagree. Independent events can still receive equal timestamps; add a deterministic tie-breaker only when a total order is required.

[Source: Lamport's original clock condition and algorithm](https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/time-clocks.pdf).

<!-- @after Reliability Considerations :: staff -->
### Test the discontinuities deliberately

Inject time into duration and expiry logic. Test a backward wall-clock adjustment, equal consecutive monotonic readings, device sleep and a new boot/process lifetime. A persisted numeric `nanoTime` deadline cannot be interpreted after restart just because it is a `Long`.

For an expiry enforced by a service, preserve the service's authority and clock policy. A client monotonic timer can improve local waiting behavior; it cannot establish the real time of a remote write or make a tampered device trustworthy.
